{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur's relationship with water makes downspouts more critical here than almost anywhere else we serve. The Tennessee River wraps around your city, Wheeler Lake sits nearby, and the water table throughout Decatur is naturally elevated. Your foundation is already working against groundwater pressure — the last thing it needs is roof runoff adding to the load.</p>

<p>Many Decatur homes have downspout problems they don't even recognize. Builder-grade systems installed with minimum-code components dump water just inches from the foundation. Basic splash blocks move it a few feet at best. In most places, that might be adequate. In Decatur, it's asking for trouble.</p>

<p>Here's what happens: during heavy rain, your roof sheds thousands of gallons of water. That water hits your gutters, flows to your downspouts, and exits — right next to your foundation. On Decatur's clay-heavy soil, that water doesn't absorb. It pools. And because your water table is already high, there's nowhere for it to go except sideways — toward your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>During TVA water releases following regional rainfall, the problem intensifies. The river rises, the water table rises with it, and foundation soil throughout Decatur becomes saturated. Homes near Point Mallard, along the Highway 31 corridor, and in low-lying areas of Southwest Decatur are particularly vulnerable. Proper downspouts with adequate extensions are your first line of defense.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system in Decatur needs three things: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and — most importantly — effective water dispersal far from your foundation.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and Decatur's rainfall intensity. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle heavy storms without backing up, which matters when you're trying to move maximum water away from your foundation quickly.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows, typically at corners and low points in your gutter system. Poorly placed downspouts create dead zones where water pools instead of draining — and in Decatur's humid climate, standing water in gutters accelerates corrosion.</p>

<p>Water dispersal is where Decatur homes need extra attention. Basic splash blocks that work elsewhere aren't adequate here. Extensions should move water at least 6-10 feet from your foundation. For many Decatur homes — especially those in low-lying areas or near the river — underground drains that route water 20+ feet away provide the protection your foundation actually needs.</p>
{% endset %}

